### time-server

- `time-server days-diff`: Calculate the day difference between two dates in "YYYY-MM-DD" format. Args: t1: Start date in "YYYY-MM-DD". t2: End date in "YYYY-MM-DD". Returns: A dict containing status and the day difference (t2 - t1) in days. JSON keys: t1, t2.
- `time-server date-to-weekday`: Convert a date string into a weekday name. Notes: - This function uses English weekday names only (locale parameter is reserved for future extension). - Input format: "YYYY-MM-DD". Args: d: Date string in "YYYY-MM-DD". locale: Reserved; currently only "en" is supported. short: If True, return abbreviated weekday (e.g., "Mon"); otherwise full name. Returns: A dict containing status and weekday name. JSON keys: d, locale, short.
- `time-server iso-seconds-diff`: Calculate the seconds difference between two ISO-8601 datetime strings. Supported examples: - "2026-01-14T12:30:00" - "2026-01-14T12:30:00Z" - "2026-01-14T12:30:00+08:00" Interpretation: - If timezone info is present, it is respected. - If timezone info is absent, the datetime is treated as naive local time. Args: t1: ISO datetime string. t2: ISO datetime string. Returns: A dict containing status and the seconds difference (t2 - t1) in seconds. JSON keys: t1, t2.
- `time-server convert-time-units`: Convert between common time units. Supported units: - "ms" (milliseconds) - "s" (seconds) - "min" (minutes) - "h" (hours) - "d" (days) - "w" (weeks) Args: value: Numeric value to convert. from_unit: Source unit. to_unit: Target unit. Returns: A dict containing status and the converted value. JSON keys: value, from_unit, to_unit.
- `time-server add-seconds-iso`: Add seconds to an ISO-8601 datetime string and return an ISO string. Notes: - If input ends with "Z", it is treated as UTC. - Output uses ISO format; if timezone-aware, includes offset. Args: t: ISO datetime string. seconds: Seconds to add (can be negative). Returns: A dict containing status and the resulting ISO datetime string. JSON keys: t, seconds.

### lang-server

- `lang-server tokenize`: Simple whitespace-and-punctuation tokenizer. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server detokenize`: Join tokens with spaces, simple heuristic. JSON keys: tokens.
- `lang-server normalize-whitespace`: Collapse consecutive whitespace into a single space and trim ends. Returns a cleaned string with normalized spacing suitable for downstream NLP tasks. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server remove-punctuation`: Remove punctuation characters from `text`, leaving words and whitespace. Useful for simple token-counting or normalization when punctuation is not needed. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server count-words`: Return the number of word tokens in `text` using the server tokenizer. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server word-freq`: Return a frequency mapping (word -> count) for tokens in `text`. Tokens are lowercased so counts are case-insensitive. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server sentence-split`: Split `text` into sentences using a simple punctuation-based heuristic. Returns a list of sentence strings (may not handle all edge cases). JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server simple-summarize`: Very small extractive summarizer by sentence length heuristic. JSON keys: text, max_sentences.
- `lang-server levenshtein`: Compute the Levenshtein edit distance between strings `a` and `b`. This implementation is memory-efficient (row-wise DP). JSON keys: a, b.
- `lang-server jaccard-similarity`: Compute Jaccard similarity between token sets of `a` and `b`. Returns a float in [0, 1], 1.0 when both inputs have identical token sets. JSON keys: a, b.
- `lang-server ngrams`: Return a list of n-grams (as space-joined strings) from `text` tokens. If there are fewer than `n` tokens an empty list is returned. JSON keys: text, n.
- `lang-server to-lower`: Return `text` converted to lowercase. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server to-upper`: Return `text` converted to uppercase. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server title-case`: Return `text` converted to title case. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server snake-case`: Convert `text` to `snake_case` by lowercasing and joining word tokens with underscores. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server camel-case`: Convert `text` to `camelCase` using word tokens (lower-first, capitalize-following). JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server slugify`: Create a URL-friendly slug from `text`: lowercase, remove punctuation, replace spaces with hyphens. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server extract-numbers`: Extract all integers and decimal numbers from `text` and return them as floats. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server hash-text`: Return a hexadecimal digest of `text` using the specified hashing `algo`. The default algorithm is `sha256`. JSON keys: text, algo.
- `lang-server base64-encode`: Encode `text` to standard Base64 and return the ASCII string. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server base64-decode`: Decode a Base64-encoded ASCII string and return the UTF-8 text. JSON keys: b64.
- `lang-server is-palindrome`: Return True if `text` is a palindrome when lowercased and alphanumerics only. Ignores punctuation and spacing when checking the palindrome property. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server regex-search`: Return all non-overlapping matches of `pattern` in `text` using `re.findall`. JSON keys: pattern, text.
- `lang-server replace`: Replace occurrences of `pattern` in `text` with `repl` using regular expressions. JSON keys: pattern, repl, text.
- `lang-server truncate`: Truncate `text` to `length` characters, appending ellipsis if truncated. JSON keys: text, length.
- `lang-server read-time-estimate`: Estimate reading time in minutes for `text` assuming `wpm` words per minute. JSON keys: text, wpm.
- `lang-server remove-stopwords`: Remove a small set of common English stopwords from `text` and return the filtered text. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server simple-paraphrase`: A tiny rule-based paraphrase that substitutes a few common words with synonyms. This is placeholder, rule-based, and not a replacement for a full paraphrasing model. JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server syllable-estimate`: Estimate the number of syllables in `word` using a simple vowel-group heuristic. JSON keys: word.
- `lang-server ordinal`: Return the ordinal string for integer `n`, e.g. 1 -> '1st', 2 -> '2nd'. JSON keys: n.
- `lang-server count-chars`: Return the number of characters in `text` (including whitespace). JSON keys: text.
- `lang-server template-render`: Render a simple Python `str.format` template using `values` mapping. JSON keys: template, values.

### crypto-server

- `crypto-server sha256`: Return the SHA-256 hex digest of the given `text`. JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server sha1`: Return the SHA-1 hex digest of the given `text`. JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server md5`: Return the MD5 hex digest of the given `text` (legacy/compatibility). JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server hmac-sha256`: Compute HMAC-SHA256 of `message` using `key`, returning a hex digest. JSON keys: key, message.
- `crypto-server pbkdf2-hex`: Derive a key from `password` and `salt` using PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256. Returns the derived key as a hex string. JSON keys: password, salt, iterations, dklen.
- `crypto-server random-bytes`: Return `n` cryptographically-secure random bytes encoded as a hex string. JSON keys: n.
- `crypto-server random-hex`: Return `nbytes` random bytes encoded as a hex string (alias of `random_bytes`). JSON keys: nbytes.
- `crypto-server base64-encode`: Encode `text` to Base64 and return the encoded string. JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server base64-decode`: Decode a Base64-encoded string and return the decoded text. JSON keys: b64.
- `crypto-server xor-cipher`: Apply a repeating-key XOR of `text` with `key` and return hex-encoded ciphertext. Note: this is not secure encryption; provided for toy/offline uses only. JSON keys: text, key.
- `crypto-server xor-dec`: Decode a hex-encoded XOR ciphertext produced by `xor_cipher` using the same `key`. JSON keys: hextext, key.
- `crypto-server simple-caesar`: Apply a simple Caesar cipher shifting alphabetic characters by `shift` positions. JSON keys: text, shift.
- `crypto-server rot13`: Return ROT13 transformation of `text` (a reversible letter substitution). JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server timing-safe-compare`: Compare two strings in a timing-safe way to mitigate timing attacks. JSON keys: a, b.
- `crypto-server derive-key`: Convenience wrapper returning a PBKDF2-derived key hex string for `password` and `salt`. JSON keys: password, salt, iterations.
- `crypto-server entropy-estimate-hex`: Estimate the Shannon entropy (bits per symbol) of hex-encoded `hexstr`. JSON keys: hexstr.
- `crypto-server uuid4`: Return a random UUID4 string.
- `crypto-server hex-to-bytes`: Convert a hex string into raw bytes. JSON keys: hexstr.
- `crypto-server bytes-to-hex`: Convert raw bytes to a lowercase hex string. JSON keys: b.
- `crypto-server urlsafe-b64-encode`: Encode `text` as URL-safe Base64 string. JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server urlsafe-b64-decode`: Decode a URL-safe Base64 string and return the decoded text. JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server hmac-sign-hex`: Return HMAC-SHA256 signature (hex) for `message` with `key`. JSON keys: key, message.
- `crypto-server hmac-verify-hex`: Verify that `signature` matches HMAC-SHA256(message,key) in a timing-safe way. JSON keys: key, message, signature.
- `crypto-server checksum-sha256-hex`: Alias for SHA-256 digest of `text` (hex string). JSON keys: text.
- `crypto-server random-choice`: Return a cryptographically-secure random element from `seq`. JSON keys: seq.
- `crypto-server secure-token-urlsafe`: Return a URL-safe secure token with the specified `length` (approx bytes). JSON keys: length.
- `crypto-server file-digest`: Compute the digest of a file at `path` using hash `algo` and return hex digest. JSON keys: path, algo.
- `crypto-server xor-bytes-hex`: XOR two equal-length hex-encoded byte strings and return hex-encoded result. JSON keys: a_hex, b_hex.

### graph-server

- `graph-server bfs`: Perform breadth-first search on `adj` from `start` and return visit order. `adj` is expected to be a mapping node -> iterable of neighbors. JSON keys: adj, start.
- `graph-server dfs`: Perform depth-first search on `adj` from `start` and return visit order. JSON keys: adj, start.
- `graph-server shortest-path-dijkstra`: Compute shortest-path distances from `source` using Dijkstra's algorithm. `adj` should map nodes to an iterable of `(neighbor, weight)` pairs. Returns a mapping node -> distance. JSON keys: adj, source.
- `graph-server is-bipartite`: Return True if graph `adj` is bipartite (2-colorable), False otherwise. JSON keys: adj.
- `graph-server connected-components`: Return a list of connected components (each a list of nodes) in `adj`. JSON keys: adj.
- `graph-server topological-sort`: Return a topological ordering of `adj` if it is a DAG; raises on cycles. JSON keys: adj.
- `graph-server mst-kruskal`: Compute a Minimum Spanning Tree using Kruskal's algorithm. `edges` is a list of `(weight, u, v)` triples and `nodes` lists all nodes. Returns a list of edges included in the MST. JSON keys: edges, nodes.
- `graph-server shortest-path-unweighted`: Compute shortest path distances in an unweighted graph (BFS distances). JSON keys: adj, source.
- `graph-server path-exists`: Return True if there exists a path from node `a` to node `b` in `adj`. JSON keys: adj, a, b.
- `graph-server adjacency-matrix`: Build an adjacency matrix for `nodes` from adjacency mapping `adj`. Returns a square matrix of 0/1 ints where entry [i][j] is 1 if there is an edge. JSON keys: adj, nodes.
- `graph-server adjacency-list-to-matrix`: Convert adjacency list mapping to an adjacency matrix using the list of keys as nodes. JSON keys: adj.
- `graph-server degree-centrality`: Return the degree (number of neighbors) for each node in `adj`. JSON keys: adj.
- `graph-server is-connected`: Return True if the graph `adj` is connected (single component). JSON keys: adj.
- `graph-server graph-complement`: Return the complement graph of `adj` over the provided `nodes`. Nodes not present in `adj` are treated as having no neighbors. JSON keys: adj, nodes.
- `graph-server random-erdos-renyi`: Generate a random undirected Erdos-Renyi graph with `n` nodes and edge probability `p`. Returns an adjacency mapping node -> list of neighbors. JSON keys: n, p.

### chem-server

- `chem-server molar-mass`: Compute an approximate molar mass for `formula` using a small periodic table. This is a naive parser that matches element symbols followed by optional integer counts. Unknown elements contribute 0.0. JSON keys: formula.
- `chem-server empirical-formula`: Return the empirical (reduced) formula for `formula` by dividing element counts by their GCD. JSON keys: formula.
- `chem-server percent-composition`: Return percent by mass composition for each element in `formula`. Percentages sum to (approximately) 100 for known elements in the small periodic table. JSON keys: formula.
- `chem-server balance-simple-reaction`: Placeholder reaction balancer: returns input mapping unchanged. This function is a stub for offline use and does not perform true balancing. JSON keys: reactants, products.
- `chem-server is-balanced`: Placeholder validator that currently always returns True for a formula's balance. JSON keys: formula.
- `chem-server ph-from-concentration`: Convert hydrogen ion concentration `h_conc` (mol/L) to pH value. JSON keys: h_conc.
- `chem-server concentration-from-ph`: Convert pH value back to hydrogen ion concentration (mol/L). JSON keys: ph.
- `chem-server ideal-gas-pressure`: Compute pressure (in SI units of kPa equivalently via ideal gas law) for given moles, temperature, and volume. Note: this uses a simple ideal gas approximation and performs unit conversions from liters. JSON keys: n_moles, temp_k, volume_l.
- `chem-server convert-moles-to-grams`: Convert an amount in `moles` of a substance with `formula` to mass in grams (approx). JSON keys: moles, formula.
- `chem-server convert-grams-to-moles`: Convert `grams` of a substance with `formula` to moles using approximate molar mass. JSON keys: grams, formula.
- `chem-server normalize-formula`: Return `formula` with whitespace removed for normalization. JSON keys: formula.
- `chem-server element-list`: Return a list of element symbols present in `formula` in the order they appear. JSON keys: formula.
- `chem-server combustion-products`: Naive estimator of complete combustion products (CO2 and H2O) from a hydrocarbon `fuel_formula`. This is a heuristic estimator that assumes integer counts and complete combustion. JSON keys: fuel_formula.
- `chem-server is-organic`: Return True if `formula` contains carbon (simple organic check). JSON keys: formula.
- `chem-server simple-smiles-validate`: Very small validator for a SMILES-like string: checks balanced parentheses only. JSON keys: smiles.
- `chem-server mole-fraction`: Compute a mole fraction given `partial_moles` and `total_moles` (returns 0 if total is zero). JSON keys: partial_moles, total_moles.
- `chem-server clamp`: Clamp `value` between `lo` and `hi` and return the clamped float. JSON keys: value, lo, hi.

